<section class="section" id="top-composers">
  <span class="section-label">Section 02</span>
  <h2 class="section-title">Most Awarded Composers</h2>
  <div class="section-body">
    <p>Three composers have reached the pinnacle of four Oscar wins each — a figure that, over 93 ceremonies, represents an extraordinary concentration of Academy recognition. Remarkably, these three span three different eras: <strong>John Barry</strong> anchors the Silver Age (1966–1990), <strong>John Williams</strong> bridges Silver into Modern (1975–1993), and <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> is rewriting the record books in real time (2018–2026, with four wins already in just eight years).</p>
    <p>Below the top tier, a cluster of three-time winners — <strong>Max Steiner, Dimitri Tiomkin, Maurice Jarre,</strong> and <strong>Alan Menken</strong> — represents the dominant voices of their respective eras. Steiner and Tiomkin are essentially the Golden Age's signature composers; Jarre defined a specific strand of sweeping, location-driven romanticism in the 1960s–80s; Menken's three-win streak in 1989–1992 remains the most concentrated period of single-composer dominance in the award's history.</p>
    <p>The two-win tier is broad and geographically diverse, including Miklos Rozsa, Franz Waxman, Alfred Newman, Howard Shore, Gustavo Santaolalla, Hans Zimmer, Alexandre Desplat, Trent Reznor, and Atticus Ross — reflecting both the longevity achievable at the top of the craft and the increasingly international character of the Contemporary era.</p>
