<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 3: TEMPORAL PATTERNS -->
<section class="section" id="temporal-patterns">
  <span class="section-label">Section 03</span>
  <h2 class="section-title">Temporal Patterns &amp; Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <div class="section-body">
    <p>Looking at the Oscar Score category across decades reveals a consistent alternation between eras of <strong>concentrated dominance</strong> and eras of <strong>diverse, first-time recognition</strong>. The 1950s stand out as the decade of highest repetition — Dimitri Tiomkin alone claimed three of ten ceremonies — while the 1980s represent the opposite pole: ten ceremonies, ten different composers, a perfect 100% uniqueness rate that no other complete decade has matched.</p>
    <p>The current decade (2020s) is on track to be the most concentrated in modern history. With seven ceremonies completed, Ludwig Göransson has won four — a 57% capture rate that, if it continues, would be unprecedented in the post-war era. His dominance is compressed into a narrow eight-year window, making it feel more intense than John Barry's four wins spread across 24 years.</p>
    <p>The data also reveals that repeat wins tend to cluster: once a composer wins, the Academy's appetite for that voice often resurfaces within a few years rather than after a long absence. Of the 26 repeat-win ceremonies, <strong>17 occurred within 10 years of a composer's previous win</strong>.</p>
  </div>

