  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 4: ERA ANALYSIS -->
<section class="section" id="era-analysis">
  <span class="section-label">Section 04</span>
  <h2 class="section-title">Era-Based Analysis</h2>
  <div class="section-body">
    <p>Segmenting the 93 ceremonies into four broad eras — derived from well-established musicological and industry-historical conventions — reveals distinct aesthetic and sociological patterns in what the Academy rewards. These eras are not hermetically sealed; a composer like John Barry spans Silver and Modern eras, and Hans Zimmer's two wins are separated by 27 years. But the era lens clarifies the dominant forces shaping each period.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="era-grid">
    <!-- GOLDEN AGE -->
    <div class="era-card" data-era="I">
      <div class="era-name">Golden Age</div>
      <div class="era-years">1934 – 1959</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Ceremonies</span><span class="era-stat-value">26</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Unique Entries</span><span class="era-stat-value">19</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Repeat Win Rate</span><span class="era-stat-value">27%</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Collaborative Wins</span><span class="era-stat-value">3</span></div>
      <div class="era-desc">The studio-system era. European-trained composers — Steiner, Korngold, Waxman, Rozsa — brought late-Romantic orchestral idioms to Hollywood. Tiomkin's three wins signal the rising appetite for Westerns. Early collaborative credits (Pinocchio, Dumbo) reflect the ensemble nature of animated scoring.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- SILVER AGE -->
    <div class="era-card" data-era="II">
      <div class="era-name">Silver Age</div>
      <div class="era-years">1960 – 1979</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Ceremonies</span><span class="era-stat-value">20</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Unique Entries</span><span class="era-stat-value">17</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Repeat Win Rate</span><span class="era-stat-value">20%</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Collaborative Wins</span><span class="era-stat-value">2</span></div>
      <div class="era-desc">The auteur era and the rise of the thematic blockbuster. Maurice Jarre's sweeping, location-steeped romanticism (Arabia, Zhivago) defines the decade's early years. John Barry's four-win career launches here. The period closes with John Williams's emergence — Jaws (1975) and Star Wars (1977) crystallizing the neo-Romantic blockbuster template that would define the next 40 years.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- MODERN -->
    <div class="era-card" data-era="III">
      <div class="era-name">Modern</div>
      <div class="era-years">1980 – 1999</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Ceremonies</span><span class="era-stat-value">20</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Unique Entries</span><span class="era-stat-value">16</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Repeat Win Rate</span><span class="era-stat-value">25%</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Collaborative Wins</span><span class="era-stat-value">1</span></div>
      <div class="era-desc">The most stylistically diverse era. Electronic scores (Vangelis, Giorgio Moroder) coexist with Broadway-influenced Disney (Alan Menken's three-peat), the continuation of Williams's blockbuster dominance, and increasing internationalism (Sakamoto/Byrne, Nino Rota, Michel Legrand). The 1980s sub-decade within this era remains uniquely diverse.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- CONTEMPORARY -->
    <div class="era-card" data-era="IV">
      <div class="era-name">Contemporary</div>
      <div class="era-years">2000 – 2026</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Ceremonies</span><span class="era-stat-value">27</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Unique Entries</span><span class="era-stat-value">21</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Repeat Win Rate</span><span class="era-stat-value">37%</span></div>
      <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Collaborative Wins</span><span class="era-stat-value">2</span></div>
      <div class="era-desc">The highest repeat-win rate of any era — driven largely by Göransson's four wins in eight years and multiple other double-winners. Geographic and stylistic diversity is nevertheless high: winners include Argentine (Santaolalla), Icelandic (Guðnadóttir), German (Bertelmann), Indian (Rahman), Chinese (Tan Dun), and Swedish (Göransson) composers. Industrial rock producers (Reznor/Ross) broke through in 2010.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 5: MILESTONES -->
<section class="section" id="notable-milestones">
  <span class="section-label">Section 05</span>
  <h2 class="section-title">Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
  <div class="section-body">
    <p>Beyond aggregate statistics, the Oscar scoring record is punctuated by singular moments — firsts, streaks, anomalies, and long-arc career narratives that reveal the human dimension of the data.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- CONSECUTIVE STREAKS -->
  <h3 style="font-size:16px;color:var(--text-primary);margin-bottom:16px;font-weight:400">Back-to-Back Winners</h3>
  <div class="streak-row">
    <div class="streak-card">
      <div class="streak-composer">Franz Waxman</div>
      <div class="streak-years">1950 – 1951</div>
      <div class="streak-films">Sunset Boulevard → A Place in the Sun</div>
    </div>
    <div class="streak-card">
      <div class="streak-composer">Alan Menken</div>
      <div class="streak-years">1991 – 1992</div>
      <div class="streak-films">Beauty and the Beast → Aladdin</div>
    </div>
    <div class="streak-card">
      <div class="streak-composer">Gustavo Santaolalla</div>
      <div class="streak-years">2005 – 2006</div>
      <div class="streak-films">Brokeback Mountain → Babel</div>
    </div>
    <div class="streak-card highlight">
      <div class="streak-composer" style="color:var(--gold)">Ludwig Göransson</div>
      <div class="streak-years">2023 – 2024</div>
      <div class="streak-films">Oppenheimer → Oppenheimer (same score, two ceremonies)</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TIMELINE OF MILESTONES -->
  <h3 style="font-size:16px;color:var(--text-primary);margin:32px 0 20px;font-weight:400">Historical Landmarks</h3>
  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">1934</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">The First Winner</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Louis Silvers</strong> won for <em>One Night of Love</em> at the 6th Academy Awards — the first ever Best Original Score Oscar. The award was called "Best Score" in early years; the category name and eligibility rules evolved significantly before stabilizing.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">1938</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Korngold and the Gold Standard</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Erich Wolfgang Korngold</strong>'s win for <em>The Adventures of Robin Hood</em> cemented the sweeping, leitmotif-driven orchestral approach as the Academy's aesthetic baseline — an influence felt throughout the Golden Age and directly traceable to John Williams's later neo-Romantic style.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">1950 – 1951</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">First Back-to-Back Win</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Franz Waxman</strong> became the first composer to win in consecutive years, taking the award for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> and then <em>A Place in the Sun</em> — a feat only three other composers (Menken, Santaolalla, Göransson) would replicate in the following 75 years.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">1975 – 1977</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">John Williams Arrives</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>John Williams</strong> won for <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977) in quick succession, establishing the blockbuster orchestral template that would define Hollywood's global commercial aesthetic. His four wins span 18 years (1975–1993), with strategic gaps that kept his influence perennial rather than exhausting.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">1989 – 1992</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Alan Menken's Disney Dominance</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Alan Menken</strong> won three times in four ceremonies (<em>The Little Mermaid, Beauty and the Beast, Aladdin</em>), driven by Disney's Renaissance period. The back-to-back wins of 1991–1992 mark the most concentrated single-composer run in Oscar scoring history. In 1989, 1990, 1991, and 1992, he was either nominated or won — a level of sustained dominance unmatched in the Modern era.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">1981</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Electronic Scores Break Through</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Vangelis</strong>'s win for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981) — following Giorgio Moroder's win for <em>Midnight Express</em> (1978) — signaled the Academy's willingness to recognize synthesizer-driven scores, a watershed moment that would influence subsequent generations of genre-crossing composers including Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">2010</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Reznor &amp; Ross — The Rock Producers Win</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross</strong> won for <em>The Social Network</em>, becoming the first winners with a background rooted in industrial rock and electronic production rather than classical composition training — a cultural signal about the Academy's broadening definition of "score."</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">2019</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">First Female Winner</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Hildur Guðnadóttir</strong> won for <em>Joker</em> in 2019, becoming the first solo female composer to win the Best Original Score Academy Award in the category's 85-year history — a milestone that highlighted the industry's long-standing gender gap in film scoring.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">2023 – 2024</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">The Oppenheimer Anomaly</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> won for <em>Oppenheimer</em> at both the 2023 and 2024 ceremonies — an artifact of the Academy's timing system whereby the same film's score appeared eligible in two successive ceremonial cycles. This is the only instance in the dataset of an identical film appearing in consecutive wins.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="milestone-year">2026</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Göransson Reaches the Summit</div>
      <div class="milestone-body"><strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong>'s win for <em>Sinners</em> at the 98th ceremony brings him to 4 career wins — tying John Williams and John Barry at the all-time record — and doing so in just 8 years (2018–2026), the most compressed four-win run in the category's history. At this pace, he is the clear favorite to set a new outright record within this decade.</div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS -->
<section class="section" id="conclusions">
  <span class="section-label">Section 06</span>
  <h2 class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
  <div class="section-body">
    <p>Nine decades of Oscar scoring data, when read together, reveal not just a list of winners but a cultural barometer — the Academy's shifting judgment about what kind of music constitutes excellence in service of storytelling. Here are the five core findings any analyst or historian should take away.</p>
  </div>

  <ul class="takeaway-list">
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">1</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>The four-win ceiling has held for 90 years — and is now under acute pressure.</strong>
        <p>John Barry reached four wins in 1990, John Williams in 1993. Neither added a fifth. For 33 years, four remained the record. Ludwig Göransson matched it in 2026 after just eight years of winning — and, at a younger career stage than either predecessor, represents the most credible threat to the record. If the 2020s sustain his current dominance, the ceiling breaks before 2030.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
      <div class="takeaway-num" style="display:none"></div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>The 1980s remain the gold standard of competitive diversity.</strong>
        <p>Ten ceremonies, ten different composers — the 1980s are the only complete decade in Oscar scoring history with a 100% unique-winner rate. This reflects both the stylistic explosion of that era (electronic, jazz, orchestral, folk) and the absence of a single dominant voice. The contrast with the 2020s — where one composer has captured 57% of ceremonies so far — could not be sharper.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">3</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>The repeat winner rate (~28%) has been remarkably stable across eras.</strong>
        <p>Whether in the Golden Age (27%), Silver Age (20%), or Modern era (25%), approximately one-in-four ceremonies rewards a returning champion. The Contemporary era (37%) is the first to meaningfully break from this pattern — driven by Göransson's four wins and multiple other contemporary double-winners. This suggests the Contemporary era's talent pool may be narrowing at the top relative to its historical predecessors.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>Internationalization is the defining structural shift of the Contemporary era.</strong>
        <p>In the Golden Age and Silver Age, winners were overwhelmingly European immigrants to Hollywood. The Modern era began opening to non-Western voices. The Contemporary era has produced winners from Argentina, Iceland, Germany, India, Japan, China, and Sweden. This is not merely cosmetic diversity — these composers bring distinct harmonic languages and instrumentation that have materially altered what an Oscar-winning score sounds like.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">5</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>Collaborative credits are returning — and the definition of "composer" is evolving.</strong>
        <p>Early collaborative wins (1939, 1941, 1942) reflected the studio-system's ensemble approach. The Modern and Silver Ages were dominated by solo auteurs. The Contemporary era has seen a modest but notable return to collaboration: Reznor/Ross, the three-person Soul team, the Godfather Part II precedent echoed in Göransson's multi-win arc. As production music and AI-assisted composition reshape the industry, the Academy's definition of authorship in scoring will face its most significant definitional challenge since the category's founding.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

